2. Who You Are Selling To
This is the most important section of this plan. You are not selling to portfolio company CEOs (at
least not initially). You are selling to the people at the PE firm level who are responsible for
driving value across the portfolio. These people have the authority to deploy you across multiple
companies simultaneously.
2.1 The Decision-Making Chain
1. AI Operating Partners (Primary Target): This is a brand new role that PE firms are
creating right now. Heidrick & Struggles reports an enormous spike in hiring for this
position. These are the people specifically tasked with deploying AI across portfolio
companies. They have budget authority and are actively building vendor ecosystems.
When they cannot find the right people, firms pull from Tier 1 consultancies with AI labs
or from centralized AI labs within financial services companies.
2. Technology / Digital Operating Partners: The established version of the above.
Already embedded in most mid-to-large PE firms. Historically focused on digital
transformation, now being asked to add AI to their mandate. Many are overwhelmed and
actively looking for implementation partners.
3. Value Creation Team Leads: The heads of the operating groups or portfolio operations
teams. They oversee all operational improvement across portfolio companies. They are
the ones who approve vendor relationships and set strategy for cross-portfolio initiatives.
4. Managing Directors / Partners with Portfolio Oversight: Senior investment
professionals who sit on portfolio company boards. They feel LP pressure directly and
push operating teams to move faster on AI. Useful allies but harder to reach cold.
5. Portfolio Company CEOs and COOs: The end users of your services. Important for
execution but typically brought in after the PE firm has made the decision to deploy AI.
HBR confirms that CEOs are telling their boards they do not know where to start with AI.
